  "text": " This is a paper by self and colleagues, MMWR, published in September in the CDC's journal. And it shows, it's an attempt to look at in community, so it's not randomized, but it looks at vaccine protection against hospitalization. And as you can see in the right hand column, I'll read those off to you. It had Moderna after 120 days at 92% protection, Pfizer at, I believe, 77% protection, and J&J at 66% protection. This is as good as you can find in community data. Now notice it's not a big sample size. We had vaccinated 150 million people. Why are they reporting on, you know, why are they reporting at 1,500 people? How did they get the sample? It wasn't that great, but I'm telling you, people got on TV, Scott Atlas, who's in part of one of my groups for Fox. Scott Atlas got on and said, you know, the vaccine protects against hospitalization and death. And in Bhadachara, who's in my group, he says that too, protects against hospitalization and death. This is what they're reporting to. This is fairly contemporary data. I could go along with this except for in the details, we didn't account for the Delta variant. Delta. So yes, did the vaccines work? I think they did against the legacy variants that are now extinct. And so we had a sign in the literature and in public health that the vaccines were failing in Delta, like on this big naval vessel where everyone's fully vaccinated and a bunch of the young kids come down with the Delta variant. And then with this outbreak analysis from Bonn Stable, Massachusetts, where it's pretty obvious you don't have to be an epidemiologist, the light blue are fully vaccinated people, the dark blue are unvaccinated. Who's got more Delta? Who's got more COVID? The vaccinated. This is CDC, MMWR. This is peer-reviewed literature. You don't have to go anywhere else. This is not misinformation. This is publicly available information published by the CDC telling you the vaccines are not working against Delta. And it was after this paper, our CDC director came out on TV and said, you know what? It looks like the vaccines aren't working for Delta. It looks like you can get COVID-19. You can transmit it. You can give it to somebody else.",
